      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788464527913,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> 100 Million DAU\n-> 1 Million RPS",
      "fontSize": 15.200000000000003,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> 100 Million DAU\n-> 1 Million RPS",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "LnBu2Pfug3aMYZuhsSJuZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 238.33341471354186,
      "y": 752.1000213623047,
      "width": 1370.759033203125,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "at",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 121252946,
      "version": 2813,
      "versionNonce": 14894345,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "kn_QgEqf7Yz-egd0V5dhd",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1788464527913,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> Server-side rate limiter, enforced at the gateway layer\n-> High availability is the priority — the rate limiter failing should not take down the system\n-> Response includes: pass/fail, remaining calls, and reset time\n-> For low latency, place the rate limiter directly inside the Load Balancer / API Gateway\n-> Alternatives: standalone service (extra network hop, more latency) or inside each microservice (no global check, duplicated logic)\n-> Gateway lacks deep business context per microservice, making fine-grained rules harder — \nsolve this by passing relevant context (user tier, role) in the JWT\n-> Different clients get different limits — e.g., authenticated users get higher limits, anonymous IPs get minimal limits\n-> Token buckets live in Redis to maintain global state across multiple gateway instances\n-> Gateway sends the check to Redis, which handles refill/consumption and returns pass/fail\n-> Race conditions are avoided via Redis's atomic Lua scripting\n-> If the limit is hit, reject immediately (no queueing) with HTTP 429\n-> At ~1M RPS, shard Redis by user ID / API key using consistent hashing — Redis Cluster handles this via hash slots\n-> If Redis/limiter fails: fail-open by default (protects overall availability), fail-closed only for high-risk endpoints like \nauth/payment where being unprotected is worse than being briefly down\n-> Use a Redis replica, promoted on primary failure, for resilience\n-> Connection pooling between gateway and Redis reduces per-request TCP overhead\n-> Rules (per-client limits, tiers) stored in a config DB or ZooKeeper, with the gateway periodically pulling updates for dynamic configuration",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> Server-side rate limiter, enforced at the gateway layer\n-> High availability is the priority — the rate limiter failing should not take down the system\n-> Response includes: pass/fail, remaining calls, and reset time\n-> For low latency, place the rate limiter directly inside the Load Balancer / API Gateway\n-> Alternatives: standalone service (extra network hop, more latency) or inside each microservice (no global check, duplicated logic)\n-> Gateway lacks deep business context per microservice, making fine-grained rules harder — \nsolve this by passing relevant context (user tier, role) in the JWT\n-> Different clients get different limits — e.g., authenticated users get higher limits, anonymous IPs get minimal limits\n-> Token buckets live in Redis to maintain global state across multiple gateway instances\n-> Gateway sends the check to Redis, which handles refill/consumption and returns pass/fail\n-> Race conditions are avoided via Redis's atomic Lua scripting\n-> If the limit is hit, reject immediately (no queueing) with HTTP 429\n-> At ~1M RPS, shard Redis by user ID / API key using consistent hashing — Redis Cluster handles this via hash slots\n-> If Redis/limiter fails: fail-open by default (protects overall availability), fail-closed only for high-risk endpoints like \nauth/payment where being unprotected is worse than being briefly down\n-> Use a Redis replica, promoted on primary failure, for resilience\n-> Connection pooling between gateway and Redis reduces per-request TCP overhead\n-> Rules (per-client limits, tiers) stored in a config DB or ZooKeeper, with the gateway periodically pulling updates for dynamic configuration",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
