    {
      "id": "c3Udt2rpEwl1jivd1kfzm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 505.81187712181134,
      "y": 311.0255948308959,
      "width": 536.9424438476562,
      "height": 235.3979814641835,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 264005319,
      "version": 1476,
      "versionNonce": 430916553,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1788465671310,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> In the UUID approach, the bits are too long and it might \ntake a lot of space which could be a problem for some.\n-> In DB autoincrement approach, only one server can be used.\nMultiple server will not generate unique IDs thus defeating\nthe whole purpose.\n-> In Ticket server approach, the ticket server acts as a \nsingle point of failure\n-> Snowflake approach is the best approach for a distributed scalable \nsystem, but it needs machine IDs and clock care. \nThis is what we will be choosing for this problem\n-> The 64 bit ID will pack timestamp bits + machine ID bits \nand per machine sequence number.\n-> Typically, 1 bit for unused sign, 41 bits for timestamp, 10bits for machine ID, 12 bits for sequence ID.\n-> These IDs are generated without any coordination and are\nroughly sortable.\n-> If current timestamp is less than last timestamp, the server refuses to generate IDs.\n-> Every ID generator node on startup gets a Unique Machine ID from the machine ID node,\ncould be a sequential counter typically with Zookeeper.",
      "fontSize": 10.462132509519266,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> In the UUID approach, the bits are too long and it might \ntake a lot of space which could be a problem for some.\n-> In DB autoincrement approach, only one server can be used.\nMultiple server will not generate unique IDs thus defeating\nthe whole purpose.\n-> In Ticket server approach, the ticket server acts as a \nsingle point of failure\n-> Snowflake approach is the best approach for a distributed scalable \nsystem, but it needs machine IDs and clock care. \nThis is what we will be choosing for this problem\n-> The 64 bit ID will pack timestamp bits + machine ID bits \nand per machine sequence number.\n-> Typically, 1 bit for unused sign, 41 bits for timestamp, 10bits for machine ID, 12 bits for sequence ID.\n-> These IDs are generated without any coordination and are\nroughly sortable.\n-> If current timestamp is less than last timestamp, the server refuses to generate IDs.\n-> Every ID generator node on startup gets a Unique Machine ID from the machine ID node,\ncould be a sequential counter typically with Zookeeper.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
